{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And one who strikes his father and his mother.</b> Why is this stated? Because when it says 'an eye for an eye,' it removed it from that rule. And this passage is stated to be stringent upon him, that he is liable to death even though he did not kill him, but only inflicted a wound upon him, as we derive from the analogy: 'one who strikes an animal' and 'one who strikes a man' — just as one who strikes an animal is not liable until he inflicts a wound upon it, so too one who strikes his father is not liable until he inflicts a wound upon him, even though it is his father or his mother; and since he has the status of a murderer, Scripture placed it adjacent to the murderer." "<b>And one who strikes his father and his mother shall surely be put to death.</b> The punishment we have heard; the warning from where? Scripture says: 'forty shall he strike him, he shall not add' — and is this not an a fortiori inference? If one who is commanded to strike is nevertheless warned not to strike [beyond the limit], then one who is not commanded to strike — is it not logical that he should be warned not to strike? [His father and his mother — the vav in place of 'or.']"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:23.019378455Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}